# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to `.env.local` and adjust. `.env.local` is git-ignored — never commit
# real credentials, and never put a secret in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable
# (those are inlined into the browser bundle).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute origin of the deployment. Used for metadata, canonical URLs, Open
# Graph tags and the URLs Stripe returns shoppers to, so it must match the
# origin the app is actually served from.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Optional overrides for the defaults in src/config/site.ts.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STORE_NAME=ZYVERO
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CURRENCY_CODE=USD

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe — server-only. Get test keys from
# https://dashboard.stripe.com/test/apikeys and keep them out of git.
#
# Placeholders only in this file. A real key belongs in .env.local (local) or
# the host's secret store (deployed), never here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Secret API key. Test keys start with `sk_test_`.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your_test_key_here

# Signing secret for the webhook endpoint (POST /api/webhooks/stripe).
# Local: `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/webhooks/stripe` prints one.
# Deployed: shown when you add the endpoint in the Stripe dashboard.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your_webhook_signing_secret_here

# Safety catch. A live key (`sk_live_…`) is refused unless this is exactly
# "true", so production credentials cannot be picked up by accident.
# STRIPE_ALLOW_LIVE_MODE=true

# No Stripe publishable key is required: payment happens on Stripe's hosted
# Checkout page and no Stripe JavaScript runs on this site.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL — server-only.
#
# Orders are persisted here. The URL usually contains a password, so it is
# never committed, never logged, and never named NEXT_PUBLIC_* (which would
# compile it into the browser bundle — the app refuses to start if it sees
# such a variable).
#
# Local development: see README → Database for creating the role and database.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DATABASE?schema=public"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Customer accounts
#
# No extra secret is required. Sessions are random 256-bit tokens stored in
# PostgreSQL as SHA-256 digests, so there is no signing key to configure and
# none to leak — see README → Accounts and authentication.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operations panel (admin) — server-only, and only ever read by a script.
#
# These are not read by the running application. They are read once, by
# `npm run admin:bootstrap`, to create the first administrator. Both are
# required and neither has a default: no administrator exists until somebody
# deliberately creates one, and no password is written down in this
# repository.
#
# Placeholders only in this file. Real values belong in .env.local (local) or
# a secret store (deployed). Never name any of these NEXT_PUBLIC_* — that
# would compile them into the browser bundle, and the script refuses to run
# if it sees a NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN* variable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=you@example.com
# ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=choose-a-long-passphrase-at-least-12-characters
# ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_NAME=Your Name

# Safety catch, mirroring STRIPE_ALLOW_LIVE_MODE: with NODE_ENV=production the
# bootstrap refuses to run unless this is exactly "true".
# ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_ALLOW_PRODUCTION=true

# Admin sessions need no secret of their own. Like customer sessions they are
# random 256-bit tokens stored as SHA-256 digests, in their own table, behind
# their own cookie (zyvero_admin_session).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Product media
#
# Where product images are stored and how their URLs are built. None of these
# is a secret and none may ever be named NEXT_PUBLIC_*: the browser is told
# the resulting URL, never which driver produced it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which storage driver to use. Defaults to "public-directory", which serves
# the images committed under public/products from this origin.
#   public-directory  files in public/products, same-origin URLs
#   cdn               a public bucket or CDN, addressed by base URL
# MEDIA_STORAGE_DRIVER=public-directory

# Required by the "cdn" driver. A plain https base URL with no credentials, no
# query and no fragment — it ends up in every rendered <img src>, so anything
# secret in it would be published. next.config.ts reads the same value and
# allows exactly that host, over https, under <base>/products/** and nothing
# else. There is no wildcard host and no allow-all.
# MEDIA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://cdn.example.com

# Lets the operations panel upload images through the public-directory driver,
# writing real files into public/products. Off by default, including in
# development: most deployments serve public/ read-only or rebuild it on every
# deploy, and an upload form that silently loses files is worse than no upload
# form. With this unset the panel says so instead of offering one.
# MEDIA_UPLOADS_ENABLED=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reserved for later steps — intentionally unused today.
# CMS, analytics, transactional email, password reset and supplier
# integrations are added in their own steps, each with its own server-only
# secrets.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
